## The Bug

The goroutines capture the **loop variable `i` by reference** (closure). Since there's only one `i` in scope, all five goroutines see the same variable. By the time any goroutine actually executes `fmt.Println(i)`, the loop has already completed and `i` is `5`.

This is the classic "loop variable capture" problem in Go prior to 1.22 (which changed loop semantics so each iteration gets its own variable).

## The Fix

Pass `i` as a parameter to the goroutine so each one gets its own copy of the value:

```go
go func(val int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(val)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(val int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(val)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

Each goroutine now receives its own `val` parameter, so the output will be `0 1 2 3 4` (in non-deterministic order due to goroutine scheduling).

